Sure, I can help you with that. Below is a simple implementation of a Connect Four game on a 4x4 board using Rust. This version will allow two players to take turns dropping their pieces into the columns until one of them gets four in a row, either horizontally, vertically, or diagonally.

```rust
use std::io;

const BOARD_SIZE: usize = 4;
const PLAYER_1: char = 'X';
const PLAYER_2: char = 'O';
const EMPTY: char = '.';

fn main() {
    let mut board = [[EMPTY; BOARD_SIZE]; BOARD_SIZE];
    let mut current_player = PLAYER_1;

    loop {
        print_board(&board);
        let column = get_column(&current_player);
        if let Some(row) = drop_piece(&mut board, column, current_player) {
            if check_win(&board, row, column, current_player) {
                print_board(&board);
                println!("Player {} wins!", current_player);
                break;
            }
            current_player = if current_player == PLAYER_1 { PLAYER_2 } else { PLAYER_1 };
        } else {
            println!("Column {} is full. Try another column.", column);
        }
    }
}

fn print_board(board: &[[char; BOARD_SIZE]; BOARD_SIZE]) {
    for row in board {
        for &cell in row {
            print!("{} ", cell);
        }
        println!();
    }
    println!("0 1 2 3");
}

fn get_column(player: &char) -> usize {
    loop {
        println!("Player {}, enter a column (0-3):", player);
        let mut input = String::new();
        io::stdin().read_line(&mut input).expect("Failed to read line");
        let input = input.trim();
        if let Ok(column) = input.parse::<usize>() {
            if column < BOARD_SIZE {
                return column;
            }
        }
        println!("Invalid input. Please enter a number between 0 and 3.");
    }
}

fn drop_piece(board: &mut [[char; BOARD_SIZE]; BOARD_SIZE], column: usize, player: char) -> Option<usize> {
    for row in (0..BOARD_SIZE).rev() {
        if board[row][column] == EMPTY {
            board[row][column] = player;
            return Some(row);
        }
    }
    None
}

fn check_win(board: &[[char; BOARD_SIZE]; BOARD_SIZE], row: usize, column: usize, player: char) -> bool {
    let directions = [
        (0, 1),  // Horizontal
        (1, 0),  // Vertical
        (1, 1),  // Diagonal /
        (1, -1), // Diagonal \
    ];

    for (dr, dc) in directions.iter() {
        let mut count = 1;
        for (d, (dr, dc)) in [(-1, -1), (1, 1)].iter().enumerate() {
            let mut r = row as isize + dr;
            let mut c = column as isize + dc;
            while r >= 0 && r < BOARD_SIZE as isize && c >= 0 && c < BOARD_SIZE as isize && board[r as usize][c as usize] == player {
                count += 1;
                if count >= 4 {
                    return true;
                }
                r += dr;
                c += dc;
            }
        }
    }
    false
}
```

This code defines a simple Connect Four game with a 4x4 board. It includes functions to print the board, get the player's input, drop a piece into the board, and check for a win condition. The game continues until one player wins or the board is full.
